      "tempo": "Act I: The GroundingAmor FatiTrack 01 of 13 Slow, Deliberate01. The Field Must BurnStyle: Contemplative Folk, Delta Blues influence \u2022 Mood: Melancholy, Resigned, Reflective, Somber but ultimately peacefulOfficial VideoDrop a note to the bardShare this trackLyrics & Chords",
      "quote": "You can shake your fist and curse the sky\nYou can fall down on your knees and cry\nBut you can't pray the rain on down\nTo save this thirsty, tired town",
      "story": "\"The Field Must Burn\" serves as the album's opening meditation on the Stoic principle of Amor Fati. It tells the story of a farmer witnessing his life's work decimated by a relentless drought. The narrative follows his emotional journey from initial despair and anger at an uncaring sky to a state of profound, if somber, acceptance. He realizes that some forces are beyond his control, and the only path forward is to embrace the harsh reality, understanding that destruction is a necessary prelude to rebirth. The song sets a contemplative and melancholic tone for the album, grounding an ancient philosophy in the tangible soil of human struggle.\n\nSparse Acoustic Guitar, Light Harmonica swells \u2022 Male, Raw, Weary, Weathered, Baritone\n\nA sparse and contemplative folk song with a heavy delta blues influence. The mood is melancholy, reflective, and deeply resigned. A raw, weary, and weathered lead male baritone vocal tells a story of loss and acceptance. The primary instrument is a simple, clean, fingerpicked acoustic guitar, accompanied by distant, mournful harmonica swells.\n\nSometimes, the hardest thing to do is simply accept what we cannot change. This song introduces the ancient concept of Amor Fati (Love of Fate) through the eyes of a farmer facing a devastating drought. He moves from anger at the sky to a grim, profound peace, realizing that nature\u2019s cycle is beyond his control. It is a meditation on letting go of the outcome to find peace in the present.\n\n\n    \n\n\n@TheShadyRiverBard",
      "clean_lyrics": "[Verse 1]\nThe cracked clay is a broken plate\nBeneath a sun of burnished brass\nThe corn stalks, just brittle bone\nLike promises that couldn't last\nMy father's sweat is in this ground\nHis father's hope in every seed\nNow all their work just blows around\nA bitter dust, a broken creed\n\n[Chorus]\nYou can shake your fist and curse the sky\nYou can fall down on your knees and cry\nBut you can't pray the rain on down\nTo save this thirsty, tired town\nSo let the fire climb up high\nAnd watch the worthless harvest die\nI finally see with sun-scorched eyes\nThe field must burn to rise\n\n[Verse 2]\nI walked the rows at break of day\nRemembered how the green would climb\nI bargained with what I can't sway\nAnd tried to turn the tide of time\nBut leaning on this fence post now\nThere's no more anger left to spend\nA quiet settles on my brow\nThis is the start, and not the end\n\n[Chorus]\nYou can shake your fist and curse the sky\nYou can fall down on your knees and cry\nBut you can't pray the rain on down\nTo save this thirsty, tired town\nSo let the fire climb up high\nAnd watch the worthless harvest die\nI finally see with sun-scorched eyes\nThe field must burn to rise\n\n[Bridge]\nThere's a life that sleeps beneath the ash\nThere's a strength the roots still hold\nThis pain is just a lightning flash\nA story that is getting old\n\n[Chorus]\nNo more shakin' fists and cursing skies\nNo more falling down with pleading cries\n'Cause you can't pray the rain on down\nTo save this thirsty, tired town\nSo let the fire climb up high\nAnd watch the worthless harvest die\nI finally see with clearin' eyes\nThe field must burn to rise\n\n[Outro]\nYeah, the field must burn\nMmm, let it burn\n\n\u00a9 2025 The Shady River Bard. All rights reserved.",
