      "tempo": "Act I: The GroundingAmor FatiTrack 02 of 13 Mid-tempo, steady walking pace02. Whatever ComesStyle: Gritty Delta Blues, Acoustic Blues Rock, Americana \u2022 Mood: Resolute, Defiant, Grounded, Unflinching, StoicOfficial VideoDrop a note to the bardShare this trackLyrics & Chords",
      "quote": "So let the river rise and fall\nI'll stand my ground and meet it all\n'Cause every scar and every grace\nIs just the pattern taking place",
      "story": "\"Whatever Comes\" expands on the album's theme of Amor Fati, shifting from the quiet resignation of the first track to an anthem of courageous acceptance. The song presents a series of vignettes\u2014losing a job, receiving a bad diagnosis, facing personal loss\u2014that represent life's unpredictable and often harsh turns. Instead of succumbing to despair, the narrator adopts a resolute stance, choosing to face each challenge as part of a larger, unavoidable pattern. Set to a gritty, mid-tempo Delta Blues rhythm, the song embodies a more active and defiant form of Stoic resilience, finding strength not in changing fate, but in the unwavering will to meet it head-on.\n\nGritty acoustic slide guitar, driving acoustic rhythm guitar, sparse bass, simple kick-snare drum beat \u2022 Male, Raw, Powerful chest voice, Weathered Baritone\n\nGritty Delta Blues with a driving Americana feel. A steady, mid. The mood is resolute, defiant, and unflinching. A powerful, weathered lead male baritone sings with grounded conviction.\n\nLife rarely asks for our permission before it changes course. This track shifts the focus to active resilience. Whether it is a pink slip, a diagnosis, or a sudden loss, the narrative explores the power of standing your ground. It isn't about liking the hardship; it's about the refusal to flinch in front of it. A declaration that we can handle the weight of our own lives.\n\n\n    \n\n\n@TheShadyRiverBard",
      "clean_lyrics": "[Verse 1]\nPink slip paper in my hand\nThe foreman wouldn't meet my eye\nSaid the bottom line is wearin' thin\nNo room for men like you and I\nFor forty years of calloused skin\nA handshake and a \"best of luck\"\nA chapter ends, a new begins\nWhether you're ready or you're stuck\n\n[Chorus]\nSo let the river rise and fall\nI'll stand my ground and meet it all\n'Cause every scar and every grace\nIs just the pattern taking place\nI'll look it right square in the face\nWhatever comes\n\n[Verse 2]\nThe white-coat doctor, sober-faced\nSpread shadows out across the screen\nA map of what cannot be erased\nThe landscape of the unforeseen\nHe talked of odds and battle plans\nBut all I heard was rushing wind\nSome things are out of any hands\nNo matter how you might pretend\n\n[Chorus]\nSo let the river rise and fall\nI'll stand my ground and meet it all\n'Cause every scar and every grace\nIs just the pattern taking place\nI'll look it right square in the face\nWhatever comes\n\n[Verse 3]\nThe front door lock don't sound the same\nThe kitchen chair sits cold and bare\nI find myself whisperin' your name\nTo silence hanging in the air\nThe world don't stop to feel your pain\nThe sun still burns, the earth still turns\nYou learn to walk inside the rain\nAnother lesson the heart learns\n\n[Chorus]\nSo let the river rise and fall\nI'll stand my ground and meet it all\n'Cause every scar and every grace\nIs just the pattern taking place\nI'll look it right square in the face\nWhatever comes\n\n[Outro]\nYeah, whatever comes\nBring it on, whatever comes\n\n\u00a9 2025 The Shady River Bard. All rights reserved.",
