      "style": "Act II: The ForgeThe Obstacle is the WayTrack 06 of 13Tempo: Mid-tempo, Driving06. Rust and Bone Gritty Americana Rock, Folk Rock, Heartland Rock \u2022 Mood: Defiant, Resilient, Communal, Hopeful, AnthemicOfficial VideoDrop a note to the bardShare this trackLyrics & Chords",
      "tempo": "Act II: The ForgeThe Obstacle is the WayTrack 06 of 13 Mid-tempo, Driving06. Rust and BoneStyle: Gritty Americana Rock, Folk Rock, Heartland Rock \u2022 Mood: Defiant, Resilient, Communal, Hopeful, AnthemicOfficial VideoDrop a note to the bardShare this trackLyrics & Chords",
      "quote": "But the rust may creep, the steel may fall\nThe bankers write us off their sheets\nBut listen close, you'll hear the call\nIn every heart that still here beats",
      "story": "\"Rust and Bone\" continues Act II's exploration of \"The Obstacle is the Way,\" expanding the focus from an individual to an entire community. Revisiting the deindustrialized landscape of the Bard's earlier work, the song portrays a town grappling with the \"rust\" of economic collapse. Instead of a lament, however, it's an anthem of collective resilience. The town's people transform their shared hardship into the \"bone\" of a new, stronger identity. The track is defined by its gritty Americana sound, a driving rhythm, a defiant fiddle, and a powerful call-and-response vocal structure that evokes the sound of a community standing and singing as one.\n\nDriving acoustic guitar, electric guitar, prominent fiddle, bass, pounding drums \u2022 Male, Raw, Gritty, Lead\n\nGritty, driving Americana rock with a heartland feel. The song is built on a powerful call, soulful lead male vocal and a small choir of gritty male backup singers. A fiery, defiant fiddle is a prominent lead instrument over a foundation of driving acoustic and electric guitars.\n\nWhat happens to a community when the factory closes? This track applies Stoic resilience to a collective scale. It paints a portrait of a town that refuses to die, transforming the \"rust\" of economic collapse into the \"bone\" of a new, harder identity. A narrative of defiant hope and the unbreakable spirit of the American worker.\n\n\n    \n\n\n@TheShadyRiverBard",
      "clean_lyrics": "[Verse 1]\nThe whistle screams a silent sound\nAcross the empty parking lots\n\nThe ghosts of progress walk this ground\nWhere promises and people rot\nThey sold the gears and locked the gate\nLeft billboards peeling in the sun\n\nSaid, \"Boys, you've come a little late,\nThe golden age is over, done.\"\n\n[Chorus]\n\nBut the rust may creep, the steel may fall\nThe bankers write us off their sheets\nBut listen close, you'll hear the call\nIn every heart that still here beats\nThey can't take the heart of it all\nThe backbone that we've always known\nWe answer when our futures call\nWe're made of rust and bone!\n\n[Verse 2]\n\nThe old men sit on folding chairs\nAnd watch the young kids paint the walls\n\nThey're breathing life in with new airs\nAnd answering their own new calls\n\nA brewery in the old machine shop\nA garden where the slag heap lay\n\nThis town's heart never skipped or stopped\nIt just learned to beat a different way\n\n[Chorus]\n\n'Cause the rust may creep, the steel may fall\nThe bankers write us off their sheets\nBut listen close, you'll hear the call\nIn every heart that still here beats\nThey can't take the heart of it all\nThe backbone that we've always known\nWe answer when our futures call\nWe're made of rust and bone!\n\n[Bridge]\nYeah, the rust, it leaves a bitter stain\nA memory of acid rain\nBut what they couldn't wash away\nIs the spine we build here every day\n\n[Chorus]\n\nTHE RUST MAY CREEP, THE STEEL MAY FALL\nTHE BANKERS WRITE US OFF THEIR SHEETS\nBUT LISTEN CLOSE, YOU'LL HEAR THE CALL\nIN EVERY HEART THAT STILL HERE BEATS\nTHEY CAN'T TAKE THE HEART OF IT ALL\nTHE BACKBONE THAT WE'VE ALWAYS KNOWN\nWE ANSWER WHEN OUR FUTURES CALL\nWE'RE MADE OF RUST AND BONE!\n\n[Outro]\n We're made of rust...  (RUST AND BONE!)\n Yeah, we're made of rust...  (RUST AND BONE!)\n What we are is...  (RUST AND BONE!)\n\n\u00a9 2025 The Shady River Bard. All rights reserved.",
