      "style": "Act III: The ReckoningMemento MoriTrack 10 of 13Tempo: Slow10. Dust to Stars Expansive Americana, Cosmic Folk, Atmospheric Folk \u2022 Mood: Contemplative, Awe-filled, Uplifting, Melancholy yet Hopeful, ReverentOfficial VideoDrop a note to the bardShare this trackLyrics & Chords",
      "tempo": "Act III: The ReckoningMemento MoriTrack 10 of 13 Slow10. Dust to StarsStyle: Expansive Americana, Cosmic Folk, Atmospheric Folk \u2022 Mood: Contemplative, Awe-filled, Uplifting, Melancholy yet Hopeful, ReverentOfficial VideoDrop a note to the bardShare this trackLyrics & Chords",
      "quote": "So don't you weep, don't you despair\nWhen the breath becomes a sigh\nThe atoms scattered on the air\nDon't ever truly, really die",
      "story": "\"Dust to Stars\" brings the album's third act to a soaring and uplifting conclusion. This expansive Americana ballad serves as the final meditation on Memento Mori, shifting the perspective from the personal to the cosmic. The song tenderly explores the idea that individual death is not an end, but a transformation\u2014a return to the universal elements from which all life is born. Musically, the track mirrors this journey, beginning as a sparse folk tune and building with layers of atmospheric pedal steel, bass, and drums into a grand, awe-inspiring soundscape. It's a song of profound comfort, reframing mortality as a beautiful and necessary part of an eternal, cosmic dance.\n\nAcoustic guitar, atmospheric pedal steel, bass, soft drums with mallets, subtle organ pads \u2022 Male, Gentle, Wise, Awe-filled, baritone\n\nAn expansive and contemplative Americana song that builds from a whisper to a cinematic climax. It starts with just a solo acoustic guitar and a gentle male vocal, gradually adding layers of atmospheric pedal steel, bass, and soft drums to create a feeling of cosmic awe. The mood is both melancholy and profoundly uplifting.\n\nA cosmic perspective on mortality. This track zooms out from the human lifespan to the timeline of the universe. It offers comfort in the physics of existence: that every atom in our bodies was forged in a star, and to the stars, we eventually return. A spiritual anthem for the scientifically minded.\n\n\n    \n\n\n@TheShadyRiverBard",
      "clean_lyrics": "[Verse 1]\nThis house of bone and blood and breath\nWas never really mine to keep\nJust borrowed for a while from death\nFrom a slumber that was long and deep\nA fragile frame, a fleeting trust\nA vessel made of sun and rain\nAnd when the final gears all rust\nIt gives itself back home again\n\n[Chorus]\nSo don't you weep, don't you despair\nWhen the breath becomes a sigh\nThe atoms scattered on the air\nDon't ever truly, really die\nThey find their way through dark and dust\nAnd mend the things that fell apart\nThey're the iron, and they are the rust\nAnd they're the beating of the heart\n\n[Verse 2]\nThe fallen leaf that feeds the root\nThe mountain crumbling to the sand\nThe bitter and the sweetest fruit\nAre part of one great, moving hand\nThe river carving out the stone\nA billion years to make a groove\nWe are not separate or alone\nIn this eternal, slow remove\n\n[Chorus]\nSo don't you weep, don't you despair\nWhen the breath becomes a sigh\nThe atoms scattered on the air\nDon't ever truly, really die\nThey find their way through dark and dust\nAnd mend the things that fell apart\nThey're the iron, and they are the rust\nAnd they're the beating of the heart\n\n[Bridge]\nAnd every atom in your hand\nWas forged inside a dying sun\nJust scattered 'cross a waiting land\n'Til your new story had begun\n\n[Chorus]\nSo look up at the endless dark\nThat diamond-scattered, ancient park\nAnd know you are that burning spark\nAnd you will never really die\nYou'll find your way through dark and dust\nAnd mend the things that fell apart\nYou are the iron, you are the rust\nAnd you're the beating of the heart\n\n[Outro]\nFrom dust to stars\n\n\u00a9 2025 The Shady River Bard. All rights reserved.",
